# PDI Processing Rules

## Scope

These rules apply whenever:
- Filling out a PDI YAML instance from source documents
- Adding new fields to a PDI YAML instance
- Updating `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_TEMPLATE.yaml`
- Updating `frontend/PDI.html`
- Updating `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_MATERIAL_AGNOSTIC_PHASE_MAPPING_PROTOCOL.md`

---

## 1. Completing a PDI Instance

### Source document → YAML

1. Name the completed instance `PDI_<material>_<date>.yaml` (ISO 8601 date).
2. Start from `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_TEMPLATE.yaml`. Never fill in the template itself.
3. Fill every field. Write `UNKNOWN` (not blank) where data is genuinely absent, and add a comment explaining why.
4. `boundary_score` must be calculated from the B-flags, not left as `0`.
5. `B10_ownership_changes` accepts `true | false | unknown` — preserve the third state.
6. All 10 event records must be individually completed before Module 3 is written.
7. Sub-processes must each have a `reason` entry explaining why they did not reach boundary threshold.

### New fields

If source data exists that has no corresponding template field:
- Add the field to the instance with a comment `# [NEW FIELD]`.
- Queue a template sync (see Section 3).
- Document every new field in the status file (see Section 2).

---

## 2. Status File (mandatory at end of every PDI session)

Always create `PDI_<material>_STATUS.md` alongside the completed YAML instance.

The status file **must** contain all four sections below. Do not omit any section even if it is empty.

```markdown
## 1. New Fields Added to PDI_TEMPLATE.yaml
<!-- One entry per new field: field name, location, YAML syntax snippet, reason added, which chain types it applies to. -->

## 2. Missing Data — High Priority
<!-- Data needed to complete a field; field is UNKNOWN or incomplete. -->
<!-- Table: ID | Missing item | YAML field affected | Impact if absent -->

## 3. Missing Data — Medium / Low Priority
<!-- Data desirable for completeness but not blocking. -->
<!-- Table: ID | Missing item | YAML field affected | Notes -->

## 4. Analyst Observations
<!-- Structural anomalies, unusual process class assignments, sub-processes that may warrant Phase elevation, protocol ambiguities, or anything a domain expert should review before the instance is used as source material for the Abstract Supply Chain Phase Template. -->
```

---

## 3. Template Sync (required after every new field)

Whenever a `[NEW FIELD]` is added to a PDI instance, all three of the following files **must** be updated in the same session before the work is considered complete:

| File | What to update |
|------|---------------|
| `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_TEMPLATE.yaml` | Add the field with blank/null default value and a comment explaining its purpose and which chain types need it. Mark optional fields clearly. |
| `frontend/PDI.html` | Add a corresponding form input (or display section) so the HTML form reflects the new field. Match the visual style of adjacent fields. |
| `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_MATERIAL_AGNOSTIC_PHASE_MAPPING_PROTOCOL.md` | Add the field to the relevant module section with the same question/description framing used throughout the document. |

If time or scope does not allow all three to be updated in the same session, record each outstanding sync as a separate item in Section 1 of the status file with the label `SYNC PENDING`.

---

## 4. Field Constraints (enforce on every instance)

| Field | Allowed values |
|-------|---------------|
| `B5_process_class` | `extractive` \| `aggregative` \| `chemical_thermal` \| `specification` \| `certification` \| `custodial` \| `commercial` |
| `B10_ownership_changes` | `true` \| `false` \| `unknown` |
| `transparency` | `high` \| `medium` \| `low` |
| `opacity_reason` | `informal_practice` \| `commercial_secrecy` \| `physical_inaccessibility` \| `multiple` \| `n/a` |
| `boundary_score` | integer 0–6, sum of B3+B4+B6+B9+B10+B11 flags |
| `opacity_score` | integer 0–4, count of true answers across C1–C4 |
| `date_completed` | ISO 8601 string e.g. `2026-04-21` |

---

## 5. Phase Count Guidance

- Fewer than 5 confirmed phases: events may have been grouped prematurely. Review Module 2.
- 6–9 phases: normal range for a well-documented chain.
- 10+ phases: flag in analyst observations. Verify each boundary independently meets scoring rules before accepting the count.

---

## 6. File Locations

| File | Path |
|------|------|
| Master template | `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_TEMPLATE.yaml` |
| HTML form | `frontend/PDI.html` |
| Markdown protocol | `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_MATERIAL_AGNOSTIC_PHASE_MAPPING_PROTOCOL.md` |
| Completed instances | `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_<material>_<date>.yaml` |
| Status reports | `frontend/docs/PDI/PDI_<material>_STATUS.md` |
